WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:07.338
<v Daniel>Welcome to the Deep Dive. I'm really glad you're joining us today because our mission for this exploration is, well, it's highly practical.</v>

2
00:00:07.739 --> 00:00:08.660
<v Maya>Extremely practical.</v>

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:22.013
<v Daniel>Right. We want to extract the absolute most important arguments and evidence about the future of work and higher education. We're talking concrete, data-backed insights that are perfect for putting straight onto a slide.</v>

4
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.375
<v Maya>Yeah, the kind of data that really reframes how people see the economy.</v>

5
00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:39.642
<v Daniel>Exactly. And to do that, we have to start with these two incredible, just wild inequality tales from the 20th century. Like if you go back to around 1915, the educational wage differential was...</v>

6
00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:44.883
<v Maya>Having a high school degree, let alone a college degree, it was a massive advantage.</v>

7
00:00:45.143 --> 00:01:00.105
<v Daniel>Right. But then that huge premium just collapsed in stages through the 1940s. It stayed roughly stable until about 1980. And then it just surged again. By 2005, the college wage premium had climbed all the</v>

8
00:01:00.145 --> 00:01:02.966
<v Daniel>way back up to its 1915 high watermark.</v>

9
00:01:03.366 --> 00:01:04.606
<v Maya>It's a complete U-shape.</v>

10
00:01:05.186 --> 00:01:08.807
<v Daniel>Which is counterintuitive to how most of us think about modern economic progress.</v>

11
00:01:09.352 --> 00:01:09.932
<v Maya>It really is.</v>

12
00:01:10.312 --> 00:01:23.957
<v Daniel>So to figure out what happened, we are using a foundational text for today's deep dive. It's this classic 2007 paper by Claudia Golden and Lawrence Katz called The Race Between Education and Technology.</v>

13
00:01:24.337 --> 00:01:27.698
<v Maya>Which eventually became chapter eight of their incredibly famous 2008 book.</v>

14
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:41.287
<v Daniel>Yeah, exactly. And we're going to stay primarily on that text today, but we'll also use a few other supporting papers to explain what they built upon and how their arguments are holding up right now. So, okay, let's unpack this.</v>

15
00:01:41.993 --> 00:01:49.377
<v Maya>Well, to understand how that wage premium swung so wildly over a century, we really first need to understand the simple mechanism driving it all.</v>

16
00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:50.057
<v Daniel>The race.</v>

17
00:01:50.417 --> 00:02:01.823
<v Maya>The race, right. Golden and Katz lay it out in very plain language. Technology is steadily raising the demand for educated workers. But meanwhile, our schooling system is raising the supply of those workers.</v>

18
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.801
<v Daniel>So it's literally a race between the two.</v>

19
00:02:03.861 --> 00:02:13.827
<v Maya>Exactly. Whichever force runs faster is what determines the wage premium. Neither side won over the whole century, but each won for these very long stretches.</v>

20
00:02:14.427 --> 00:02:24.693
<v Daniel>I always like to picture this. It's like running on a treadmill. Technology is the dial increasing the speed of the belt. So that's the demand. And our education system is our running pace, the supply.</v>

21
00:02:25.093 --> 00:02:26.794
<v Maya>That is a brilliant way to frame it.</v>

22
00:02:27.153 --> 00:02:36.420
<v Daniel>All right, so if we run faster than the belt, the premium falls. We have plenty of talent. But if the belt outpaces our running, the premium rises because those skills are scarce.</v>

23
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:46.087
<v Maya>Precisely. And they have the exact numbers for this race. Over the period from 1915 to 2005, the relative supply of college workers grew about 2.87% a year.</v>

24
00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:49.114
<v Daniel>Which is pretty fast.</v>

25
00:02:49.434 --> 00:03:03.522
<v Maya>It is. And over that whole century-long span, that 2.87% roughly matched the growth in demand. In their preferred estimate, a 10% rise in the relative supply of college equivalents lowers the college wage premium by 6.1%.</v>

26
00:03:04.743 --> 00:03:08.305
<v Daniel>So a 10% supply bump drops the premium by about 6%.</v>

27
00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:20.555
<v Maya>Yes, which gives us an elasticity of substitution of about 1.64, meaning employers have some flexibility to swap college and non-college workers, but not an infinite amount.</v>

28
00:03:21.316 --> 00:03:31.425
<v Daniel>Okay, but doesn't this canonical model rely on, like, treating all college graduates as this one homogenous group? I mean, it completely ignores the specific skills inside a degree, right?</v>

29
00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:40.914
<v Maya>That is absolutely the strongest objection to the model. And it's a huge point. We are definitely going to explore that later with some newer data because the nature of a degree has changed.</v>

30
00:03:41.274 --> 00:03:50.880
<v Daniel>Right. But sticking to the historical record for a second, since the century-long average was basically a tie, we need to break down those specific eras. When did education actually win?</v>

31
00:03:51.710 --> 00:04:06.399
<v Maya>Education won massively early on. From 1915 to 1940, supply grew at 3.19% a year, completely outrunning demand, which was only at 2.27%. And they kept winning. From 1940 to 1960,</v>

32
00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:18.786
<v Maya>supply grew 2.63% against 1.79% for demand. The historical proof that an education system can win this race is the massive high school movement that started just after 1910.</v>

33
00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:22.057
<v Daniel>When communities just decided to build high schools everywhere.</v>

34
00:04:22.137 --> 00:04:28.879
<v Maya>Exactly. But we also have to note that market forces weren't completely alone in this. The institutions mattered.</v>

35
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.320
<v Daniel>Right. Like policy choices.</v>

36
00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:43.665
<v Maya>Yeah. The wage compression in the 1940s went beyond just supply and demand. It was heavily influenced by wartime wage policy and the strength of unions, just like how late 1970s minimum wage factors and unions also played a role.</v>

37
00:04:44.238 --> 00:04:53.768
<v Daniel>Okay, so education was keeping up. Institutions were doing their part. What changed after 1980? Because that's when the treadmill belt just seems to launch people off the back.</v>

38
00:04:54.088 --> 00:05:02.957
<v Maya>It really does. And what changed is that our relative supply growth slowed down. It dropped from 3.77% to only about 2% a year.</v>

39
00:05:03.297 --> 00:05:04.899
<v Daniel>So we just stopped running as fast.</v>

40
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:16.785
<v Maya>Exactly. Had supply kept its 1960 to 1980 pace, Golden and Katz show the premium would have actually fallen. Instead, it rose about 0.9 percent a year.</v>

41
00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:27.329
<v Daniel>You know, whenever this post-1980 rise in the premium comes up, people immediately want to blame immigration. They assume a bunch of unskilled labor entered the market and skewed the numbers.</v>

42
00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:35.993
<v Maya>Which is largely a myth in this context. The data shows immigration explains only about 10 percent of the post-1980 rise in the wage premium.</v>

43
00:05:36.345 --> 00:05:37.186
<v Daniel>Only 10%.</v>

44
00:05:37.486 --> 00:05:42.529
<v Maya>Right. The slowdown in college going among the native born was about nine times more important.</v>

45
00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:57.379
<v Daniel>So it was a homegrown supply problem. But wait, here's my objection to this macro view. It kind of assumes a degree is this static asset. Doesn't a slowdown in supply sort of mask the fact that</v>

46
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.063
<v Daniel>the type of education matters? Like maybe applied skills were starting to depreciate differently.</v>

47
00:06:03.686 --> 00:06:16.393
<v Maya>Yes, that is highly relevant, especially as we get closer to the modern era. But what's fascinating here is Golden and Katz's famous quip about that post-1980 era. They literally wrote, it's not technology, stupid.</v>

48
00:06:17.073 --> 00:06:18.214
<v Daniel>I love that.</v>

49
00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:26.858
<v Maya>Right. The massive inequality wasn't because tech suddenly got unimaginably faster. It was because our education system just slowed down.</v>

50
00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:40.219
<v Daniel>Okay, so if the race framework perfectly explains everything up to 2005, we really must extend the timeline. We need to see if this model holds up against the arrival of advanced computerization and AI.</v>

51
00:06:40.679 --> 00:06:41.140
<v Maya>Absolutely.</v>

52
00:06:41.837 --> 00:06:56.251
<v Daniel>So Katz and Murphy had a 1992 framework that established the supply and demand model for the 1963 to 1987 period. They showed the premium track fluctuations in college supply against steady demand.</v>

53
00:06:56.511 --> 00:06:56.712
<v Maya>Right.</v>

54
00:06:57.432 --> 00:07:03.038
<v Daniel>But then we have this 2020 update by Otter, Golden, and Katz, and they extend the race all the way from 1825 to 2017.</v>

55
00:07:05.777 --> 00:07:17.439
<v Maya>And what they found is that the elasticity remains incredibly steady at about 1.62. But the log college premium rose 0.274 from 1979 to 2017.</v>

56
00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:23.481
<v Daniel>While a running pace, the relative supply growth, fell from 3.1 to 2.13 percent a year.</v>

57
00:07:23.781 --> 00:07:29.802
<v Maya>Exactly. And that supply slowdown accounts for an astonishing 62 percent of the acceleration in the premium.</v>

58
00:07:30.518 --> 00:07:44.845
<v Daniel>Just to put that slow down in perspective, educational attainment rose 0.79 years per decade for cohorts born from 1876 to 1951. But for cohorts born after 1951, it only rose 0.29</v>

59
00:07:45.445 --> 00:07:47.787
<v Daniel>years per decade. We just stalled.</v>

60
00:07:48.287 --> 00:08:01.137
<v Maya>We did. But, and this is crucial, there is a massive puzzle here flagged by the authors themselves. Trend demand growth for college workers actually slows down starting in the early 1990s.</v>

61
00:08:01.557 --> 00:08:03.799
<v Daniel>Wait, it slows down in the 90s?</v>

62
00:08:04.019 --> 00:08:04.299
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

63
00:08:05.461 --> 00:08:13.348
<v Daniel>Here's where it gets really interesting because the 90s is the dawn of the commercial Internet. It's the tech boom. And you're saying the demand for college workers slowed.</v>

64
00:08:13.833 --> 00:08:26.644
<v Maya>That's the puzzle. Furthermore, the college group became much more heterogeneous. The graduates of highly selective schools and those with post-BA degrees, they started doing far, far better than the rest of the college graduates.</v>

65
00:08:27.004 --> 00:08:41.456
<v Daniel>So the canonical model really needs help right when AI and modern tech arrive? I mean, the rapid and disruptive technological change from computerization, robots, and AI is just not to be found in this basic two-factor college versus non-college model.</v>

66
00:08:41.734 --> 00:08:54.722
<v Maya>It really isn't. The numbers bear that out perfectly. Education differentials explain 75% of the rising wage inequality from 1980 to 2000, but they only explain 38% of it from 2000 to 2017. Wow,</v>

67
00:08:55.442 --> 00:09:09.270
<v Daniel>only 38%. Which points to the strongest objection to the whole framework today. This old model might be breaking down entirely because AI just isn't skill biased in the traditional sense. It doesn't neatly favor college over non-college anymore.</v>

68
00:09:09.747 --> 00:09:20.930
<v Maya>Exactly. Because this two-factor model is struggling to map the modern tech boom, we really need to look at the absolute latest data on AI's impact on recent graduates. We have to peek inside the degrees themselves.</v>

69
00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:29.312
<v Daniel>Okay. Let's do a 2026 reality check then. Because right now, everyone wants to know if AI is displacing the newest grads.</v>

70
00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:44.143
<v Maya>To do that, we can look at a very recent paper by Fairley and Wu from September 2026. They used CPS data, the current population survey, specifically for June to August 2026 to check if the bottom was falling out for new graduates.</v>

71
00:09:44.443 --> 00:09:45.164
<v Daniel>And what did they find?</v>

72
00:09:45.724 --> 00:09:54.472
<v Maya>Well, the unemployment findings were pretty clear. Unemployment among recent college grads did not spike relative to earlier summers, older grads or young non-grads.</v>

73
00:09:54.672 --> 00:09:56.093
<v Daniel>Even with all the AI disruption?</v>

74
00:09:56.334 --> 00:10:08.922
<v Maya>Yeah. And even when they broadened it by adding people who are technically outside the labor force, but say they want a job, which adds nearly two full points to the rate, there is still no significant 2026 increase.</v>

75
00:10:09.163 --> 00:10:22.373
<v Daniel>Okay, I have to offer an analogy here just to push back a bit. Isn't looking at this very early data and declaring new grads are safe from AI a bit like looking out the window on a sunny Monday and declaring winter is canceled?</v>

76
00:10:22.794 --> 00:10:24.475
<v Maya>That is a very fair critique.</v>

77
00:10:24.773 --> 00:10:35.495
<v Daniel>Right. I mean, it's extremely early. It's survey based. And crucially, it's measuring unemployment, not wages. Companies might just suppress entry level wages before they actually stop hiring.</v>

78
00:10:35.975 --> 00:10:48.837
<v Maya>I agree completely. And the authors know the limitations, too. The data can't predict the five year wage trend. But what it can show is that there is no immediate, catastrophic mass displacement happening right at the second.</v>

79
00:10:49.077 --> 00:10:53.438
<v Daniel>OK, fair enough. So if the displacement isn't immediate, what is actually happening?</v>

80
00:10:53.957 --> 00:11:02.661
<v Maya>That's where we bring in this brilliant 2020 paper by Deming and Nore. They show that it's no longer just a question of more college. It's about the race within a degree.</v>

81
00:11:03.182 --> 00:11:04.282
<v Daniel>The race within a degree.</v>

82
00:11:04.562 --> 00:11:13.047
<v Maya>Yes. They used millions of job ads and they found that jobs for computer science, engineering and business grads change their required skills the fastest.</v>

83
00:11:13.247 --> 00:11:17.769
<v Daniel>So those STEM and business degrees, the ones everyone pushes, they are changing rapidly.</v>

84
00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:29.137
<v Maya>very rapidly. Now, they do earn the biggest premium right at entry, but because the skills change so fast, that premium declines quickly with age, and those workers actually exit those occupations faster.</v>

85
00:11:29.377 --> 00:11:31.078
<v Daniel>Wait, really? They leave STEM?</v>

86
00:11:31.318 --> 00:11:39.703
<v Maya>Yeah. Higher ability workers are five points more likely to be in STEM at age 24, but they are no more likely to be in STEM at age 40 than anyone else.</v>

87
00:11:40.255 --> 00:11:51.840
<v Daniel>So bringing a century of historical data and this immediate 2026 reality check together. So what does this all mean? How do institutions built on this old 20th century model adapt?</v>

88
00:11:52.530 --> 00:12:07.463
<v Maya>We have to separate the evidence really clearly here. Golden and Katz were absolutely right that the supply of skills matters as much as technology. Education policy is wage policy. And the idea that organizes the whole debate is still the race itself.</v>

89
00:12:08.024 --> 00:12:22.855
<v Maya>However, the 2026 evidence complicates things. AI may not be skill biased in the old sense. Demand growth for the average grad actually slowed decades ago. And as we just saw, specific skills inside a degree depreciate really</v>

90
00:12:22.895 --> 00:12:23.275
<v Maya>quickly.</v>

91
00:12:23.975 --> 00:12:34.100
<v Daniel>So let's translate this into concrete takeaways for universities. Like, what is the right supply response when the demand shift from AI is just totally uncertain?</v>

92
00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:44.744
<v Maya>It means universities must teach skills that depreciate slowly, like critical thinking and adaptability, right alongside the applied technical ones. You can't just teach the software of the day.</v>

93
00:12:45.502 --> 00:12:53.507
<v Daniel>There has to be a dire need for lifelong reentry, right, for adult learners. Because if your applied skills depreciate in five years, you have to go back.</v>

94
00:12:53.768 --> 00:13:01.112
<v Maya>Exactly. Universities must find entirely new ways to credential those skills and partner with employers to keep that continuous loop going.</v>

95
00:13:01.513 --> 00:13:06.636
<v Daniel>Which brings up a really important inference. We have to consider who is most exposed to this risk.</v>

96
00:13:07.363 --> 00:13:21.072
<v Maya>Yes. And to be clear, this is an inference, not a direct finding of the specific papers, but it's vital. Early career workers, adult learners, and first-generation students carry the absolute heaviest cost when a degree's entry premium fades.</v>

97
00:13:21.593 --> 00:13:24.955
<v Daniel>Because they don't have the safety net to just weather the depreciation of their skills.</v>

98
00:13:25.155 --> 00:13:25.636
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

99
00:13:26.896 --> 00:13:36.383
<v Daniel>And if we connect this to the bigger picture, remember who won the race historically. The historical winner was the public expansion of schooling through the high school movement.</v>

100
00:13:37.117 --> 00:13:43.681
<v Maya>So a public university system is the natural point of leverage today. It's the only infrastructure big enough to handle this.</v>

101
00:13:44.141 --> 00:13:58.549
<v Daniel>It really is. You know, it leaves you with a final thought to really mull over. If the race is no longer just against technology, but actually against the rapid depreciation of our own specialized skills,</v>

102
00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:04.232
<v Daniel>perhaps the most valuable degree of the future isn't the one that teaches a specific trade.</v>

103
00:14:04.432 --> 00:14:04.652
<v Maya>Right.</v>

104
00:14:05.832 --> 00:14:12.620
<v Daniel>Maybe the best degree is the one that fundamentally teaches a student how to unlearn and relearn on a constant loop.</v>
